  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel is being tested. Its documented rule says: \"The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.\" The two reply texts are, in full, {\"value\": \"Hello\", \"span\": \"hello\", \"document\": \"hello\"} and {\"value\": \"hello\", \"span\": \"hello\", \"document\": \"hello\"}. For the first, the rule requires VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN, because no relaxation is in force and the value-in-span check is case-sensitive, so \"Hello\" is not in \"hello\". For the second, the rule requires GROUNDED, because \"hello\" is in \"hello\". The check gives GROUNDED for both, because it casefolds the value and span before testing containment. What would make me wrong: if the harness's own value-in-span check is defined to be case-insensitive, or if \"no relaxation\" does not govern the value-in-span check, then the rule would not require the first to be VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN and the pair would not be a find.",
